     1          % =========================================================================
     2          % CHATKT - the XMSG KERNEL send/receive path, in isolation, by loopback.
     3          %
     4          % WHY THIS EXISTS. 2026-09-01: the repeat-first-message bug survives three
     5          % verified-deployed fixes, and the two halves nobody has ever tested are
     6          % the KERNEL-facing ones. CHATCTST proved the client's cache, paint math
     7          % and parse clean - but it wrote inBuf directly, skipping xmpfrea. The
     8          % server's buildFromSlot/broadcast were read, not run. What neither side
     9          % has ever measured is what the XMSG kernel itself does with the exact
    10          % call sequences the product uses:
    11          %
    12          %   the server sends with   xmpfget / xmpfwri(0,0,...) / xmpsend(2**XFSEC)
    13          %   the client receives with xmpfrcv / clamp / xmpfrea(0,0,...) / xmpfrel
    14          %
    15          % xmpfwri and xmpfrea act on the CURRENT message (they take no message
    16          % identifier). The guide says XMPFRCV makes the ARRIVAL task current, and
    17          % XMPFWRI writes into the TASK CURRENT message - but the XMPFGET page
    18          % NEVER SAYS the fresh buffer becomes current. CHATSV's own comment claims
    19          % it does; that is a claim, not a measurement. If it does not, xmpfwri
    20          % writes the reply somewhere else and xmpsend ships a NEVER-WRITTEN pool
    21          % buffer - stale content, which is exactly the live symptom's shape.
    22          %
    23          % THE METHOD: open one port, send to our own magic number, read back.
    24          % No server, no second machine, no screen. Every message has a DIFFERENT
    25          % letter and a DIFFERENT length, so a stale buffer shows up twice over -
    26          % wrong first byte AND wrong nBytes.
    27          %
    28          % HOW TO READ IT: every line starts PASS or FAIL and the last line is a
    29          % count. A FAIL in phase 3 with phases 1-2 clean means the kernel writes
    30          % the reply into the HELD ARRIVAL instead of the fresh buffer - the bug
    31          % is found, and the fix is xmpwrte (write BY IDENTIFIER) in sendTo.
    32          % =========================================================================
    33          MODULE chatkt
    34          
    35          % The real COSMOS binding, INSIDE the module - above MODULE the
    36          % declarations land in the outer scope and every call fails
    37          % NOT PREVIOUSLY DECLARED while the include lines look accepted.
    38          $INCLUDE XMP-B02:DEFS
